"O'Bryant §5.7: the golden minimizer | trureturing (https://www.trureturing.org/research/obryant-2024-golden-minimizer-refutation/)\nciteturn9search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Kevin O'Bryant, Visualizing the Sum-Product Conjecture, arXiv:2411.08139v2, §5.7 asks: ... The counterexample uses the real root r in (1,2) of r³ − r − 1 = 0 and A = {r^e : e ∈ {0,3,5,6,7,8,9}}. ... Literature: the latest source for the question is arXiv:2411.08139v2. arXiv:2601.21828v3 concerns integer sets and does not settle this positive-real minimizer question; arXiv:2605.28781 concerns asymptotic sum-product behavior and does not settle the finite claim. `formal-conjectures/ErdosProblems/52.lean` states the main conjecture only and does not settle this §5.7 minimizer assertion.\n\n# O'Bryant §5.7: the golden minimizer\n\nLiterature status: not rechecked Repository record: refuted\n\nResolution source: D5/S3/Arith/SumProductGoldenMinimizerRefutation.result10.48550/arXiv.2411.08139Source dossierRelease 36a49830636b\n\nProblem\n\n## The question\n\nKevin O'Bryant, Visualizing the Sum-Product Conjecture, arXiv:2411.08139v2, §5.7 asks:\n\n> What is the smallest possible value of |A + A|, where A is a set of n positive real numbers with |AA| ≤ 3n − 4? Is it achieved by {φ^i : 1 ≤ i ≤ n}, where φ = (1 + √5)/2?\n\nThe Lean `claim` retains the universal quantifiers over n and finite positive real sets and compares the pointwise sumsets. The theorem `result : ¬ claim` refutes this universal minimizer assertion.\n\nMotivation\n\n## Our foothold\n\nThe question proposes the consecutive golden powers as a universal minimizer under a product-cardinality constraint. A single seven-element positive-real set with no more than seventeen products and fewer sums disproves it.\n\nGap\n\n## Missing bridges\n\nThe counterexample uses the real root r in (1,2) of r³ − r − 1 = 0 and A = {r^e : e ∈ {0,3,5,6,7,8,9}}. The product set is covered by the sixteen exponent sums. The relation r³ = r + 1 gives a coefficient-vector cover of A + A by twenty-two values. The geometric seven-term lower bound gives at least twenty-four sums for the golden set at n = 7.\n\nRoute\n\n## Proposed approach\n\nThe intermediate value theorem supplies r with 1 < r < 2 and r³ = r + 1. Strict monotonicity of powers gives seven distinct elements. Pointwise products map into the image of E + E, whose computed cardinality is 16. For sums, the seven powers are evaluated from seven integer coefficient vectors; the vector sumset has cardinality 22 and evaluation preserves the required inclusions. The existing geometric lower bound is applied with both scale and ratio equal to the golden ratio.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nO'Bryant's seven-point positive-real sum-product cell | trureturing (https://www.trureturing.org/research/obryant-2024-sum-product-seven-point-cell/)\nciteturn9search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Kevin O'Bryant, Visualizing the Sum-Product Conjecture, arXiv:2411.08139v2, §5.2: ... Its theorem `result : claim` follows from the stronger statement\n\n# O'Bryant's seven-point positive-real sum-product cell\n\nLiterature status: not rechecked Repository record: proved\n\nResolution source: D5/S3/Arith/SumProductSevenPointCell.result10.48550/arXiv.2411.08139Source dossierRelease 36a49830636b\n\nProblem\n\n## The question\n\nKevin O'Bryant, Visualizing the Sum-Product Conjecture, arXiv:2411.08139v2, §5.2:\n\n> We believe that (23, 13) is not in SPP_{R+}(7).\n\nFor a domain X, the spectrum is\n\n$$ \\operatorname{SPP}_X(n) = {(|A+A|,|AA|): A\\subseteq X,\\ |A|=n}. $$\n\nHere R+ denotes the strictly positive reals, and A+A and AA denote the pointwise sumset and product set. The exact assertion is that every finite set A of positive reals with seven elements satisfies\n\n$$ \\neg\\bigl(|A+A|=23\\ \\land\\ |AA|=13\\bigr). $$\n\nThe Lean `claim` retains these quantifiers and hypotheses. Its theorem `result : claim` follows from the stronger statement\n\n$$ |AA|=13\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad |A+A|\\geq24. $$\n\n## Our foothold\n\nThe least possible product cardinality is 2|A|−1. At equality, positivity forces a geometric progression, so a specific corner of the sum-product spectrum becomes a problem about collisions between sums of powers of one real parameter. The bound excludes the proposed cell and identifies the sharp minimum of the sum cardinality along this product-minimal boundary.\n\n## Missing bridges\n\nTier 1, externally stated in arXiv:2411.08139v2 §5.2 and preregistered in issue #14745. The supplied literature scope lists the integer small-set papers arXiv:2307.06874 and arXiv:2601.21828, the asymptotic paper arXiv:2605.28781, and `ErdosProblems/52.lean` in formal-conjectures, which states the main sum-product conjecture. These references do not themselves supply a proof of this seven-element positive-real cell exclusion. The claimed absence of a subsequent settlement within that scope is `ASSUMED-UNVERIFIED` here: the literature information was supplied with the task and was not independently browsed.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nVisualizing the Sum-Product Conjecture | ArxivLens (https://arxivlens.com/paperview/details/visualizing-the-sum-product-conjecture-2532-d74325d2)\nciteturn9search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Crawled: last week; This paper computes the exact values for $SPP(n)$ for $n\\le 6$ and a substantial subset for $n\\le 32$, presenting a dataset of 1,158,717 sets that demonstrate additive and multiplicative diversity without supporting evidence for Erdős's Sum-Product Conjecture. ... @article{obryant2024visualizing, title = {Visualizing the Sum-Product Conjecture}, author = {O'Bryant, Kevin}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2411.08139}, archivePrefix = {arXiv}, primaryClass = {math.NT}, }\n\nBack ArXiv math.NT\n\nArXiv math.NT math.CO 11B13\n\n# Visualizing the Sum-Product Conjecture\n\nKevin O'Bryant\n\n2411.08139 Published Nov 12, 2024\n\nRead paper [Button: Cite ] More tools\n\nChat with paper 中文 View PDF Share\n\nOverview Analysis Cite Related research Discussion\n\nAt a glance AI-generated · verify against the paper\n\nThis paper computes the exact values for $SPP(n)$ for $n\\le 6$ and a substantial subset for $n\\le 32$, presenting a dataset of 1,158,717 sets that demonstrate additive and multiplicative diversity without supporting evidence for Erdős's Sum-Product Conjecture. The study includes visualizations and proposes several conjectures and open problems.\n\n## Abstract\n\nLet $SPP(n)$ be the set $\\{\\big(|A+A|,|A\\cdot A|\\big) : A\\subseteq {\\mathbb N}, |A|=n \\}$, where $A+A$ is the sumset $\\{a+b : a,b\\in A\\}$ and $AA$ is the product set $\\{ab : a,b\\in A\\}$. We prove the value of $SPP(n)$ exactly for $n\\le 6$, and compute a large subset of $SPP(n)$ for $n\\le 32$. This is a dataset of 1,158,717 sets of positive integers that are addtiively and multiplicatively diverse. We do {\\bf not} see evidence in favor of Erd\\H{o}s's Sum-Product Conjecture in our dataset. We include a number of conjectures, open problems, and observations motivated by this dataset, and a large number of color visualizations.\n\n### How to Cite This Paper\n\n[Button: BibTeX] [Button: APA] [Button: MLA] [Button: Chicago] [Button: IEEE]\n\n@article{obryant2024visualizing, title = {Visualizing the Sum-Product Conjecture}, author = {O'Bryant, Kevin}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2411.08139}, archivePrefix = {arXiv}, primaryClass = {math.NT}, }\n\nReference manager: RIS EndNote\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2412.21194] On the clique number of random Cayley graphs and related topics (https://slimjectvitamins.com/accel-vitamin-b-complex-capsules/?_=%2Fabs%2F2412.21194%23CD3DgejCa2b3OIySVEszRQc%3D)\nciteturn9search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; [Submitted on 30 Dec 2024] ... As a further application of this result, we study a conjecture of Alon stating that every group of order $N$ has a Cayley graph whose clique number and independence number are both $O(\\log N)$, proving the conjecture for all abelian groups of order $N$ for almost all $N$. ... Cite as:  | arXiv:2412.21194 [math.CO]\n   | (or arXiv:2412.21194v1 [math.CO] for this version)\n\n# Mathematics > Combinatorics\n\n[Submitted on 30 Dec 2024]\n\n# Title:On the clique number of random Cayley graphs and related topics\n\nAuthors:David Conlon, Jacob Fox, Huy Tuan Pham, Liana Yepremyan\n\nView a PDF of the paper titled On the clique number of random Cayley graphs and related topics, by David Conlon and 3 other authors\n\nView PDF HTML (experimental)\n\n> Abstract:We prove that a random Cayley graph on a group of order $N$ has clique number $O(\\log N \\log \\log N)$ with high probability. This bound is best possible up to the constant factor for certain groups, including~$\\mathbb{F}_2^n$, and improves the longstanding upper bound of $O(\\log^2 N)$ due to Alon. Our proof does not make use of the underlying group structure and is purely combinatorial, with the key result being an essentially best possible upper bound for the number of subsets of given order that contain at most a given number of colors in a properly edge-colored complete graph. As a further application of this result, we study a conjecture of Alon stating that every group of order $N$ has a Cayley graph whose clique number and independence number are both $O(\\log N)$, proving the conjecture for all abelian groups of order $N$ for almost all $N$. For finite vector spaces of order $N$ with characteristic congruent to $1 \\pmod 4$, we prove the existence of a self-complementary Cayley graph on the vector space whose clique number and independence number are both at most $(2+o(1))\\log N$. This matches the lower bound for Ramsey numbers coming from random graphs and solves, in a strong form, a problem of Alon and Orlitsky motivated by information theory.\n\nSubjects:  | Combinatorics (math.CO); Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM); Number Theory (math.NT); Probability (math.PR)\nCite as:  | arXiv:2412.21194 [math.CO]\n   | (or arXiv:2412.21194v1 [math.CO] for this version)\n   | https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2412.21194 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\n\n## Submission history\n\nFrom: Huy Tuan Pham [view email]\n[v1] Mon, 30 Dec 2024 18:57:48 UTC (44 KB)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nErdős's sum-product conjecture for positive integers | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/365769/erdos-s-sum-product-conjecture-for-positive-integers)\nciteturn9search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Kevin O'Bryant, “Visualizing the Sum-Product Conjecture”, arXiv:2411.08139 (2025).\n\n# Erdős's sum-product conjecture for positive integers\n\n2 years old · traced to 2024\n\nLet $A$A be a finite set of positive integers, and write\n\n$$A+A=\\{x+y:x,y\\in A\\},\\qquad AA=\\{xy:x,y\\in A\\}.$$A+A={x+y:x,y∈A},AA={x y:x,y∈A}.\n\nErdős's sum-product conjecture. For every $\\epsilon>0$ϵ>0 there is an $n_0$n 0​ such that, whenever $|A|\\ge n_0$∣A∣≥n 0​,\n\n$$\\big|(A+A)\\cup(AA)\\big|\\ge |A|^{2-\\epsilon}.$$​(A+A)∪(AA)​≥∣A∣2−ϵ.\n\nThis is the classical sum-product conjecture, originating with Erdős and later substantially advanced by Erdős and Szemerédi. The source presents equivalent formulations using sum-product pairs and notes that the conjecture is not supported by the finite dataset examined; its general status remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n  1. Kevin O'Bryant, “Visualizing the Sum-Product Conjecture”, arXiv:2411.08139 (2025).\n\nadditive-combinatoricsnumber-theorysum-product-phenomena\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.-------