jecture for normalized sum-product pairs | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/365772/limit-point-conjecture-for-normalized-sum-product-pairs)\nciteturn9search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Kevin O'Bryant, “Visualizing the Sum-Product Conjecture”, arXiv:2411.08139 (2025).\n\n# Limit-point conjecture for normalized sum-product pairs\n\nAbout 2 years old · traced to 2024\n\nLet $\\operatorname{NSPP}([3,\\infty))$NSPP([3,∞)) denote the multiset of normalized sum-product pairs for positive-integer sets of cardinalities at least $3$3, with each pair lying in $[1,2]^2$[1,2]2. The relevant sum-product coordinates are normalized so that the extremal value $2$2 corresponds to quadratic-scale growth. Limit-point conjecture for normalized sum-product pairs. The limit points of\n\n$$\\operatorname{NSPP}([3,\\infty))$$NSPP([3,∞))\n\nare confined to the two line segments\n\n$$\\{2\\}\\times[1,2]\\quad\\text{and}\\quad [1,2]\\times\\{2\\}.$${2}×[1,2]and[1,2]×{2}.\n\nThis is the normalized geometric formulation of the Erdős sum-product conjecture: asymptotically, normalized pairs should move north and east. The source gives no proof or disproof of this formulation.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nKevin O'Bryant, “Visualizing the Sum-Product Conjecture”, arXiv:2411.08139 (2025).\n\nadditive-combinatoricsasymptotic-combinatoricssum-product-phenomena\n\n0 0\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nau:Conlon_D in:cs - SciRate Search (https://scirate.com/search?q=au%3AConlon_D+in%3Acs)\nciteturn9search7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Dec 31 2024 math.CO cs.DM math.NT math.PR arXiv:2412.21194v1 ... @misc{2412.21194, author = {David Conlon and Jacob Fox and Huy Tuan Pham and Liana Yepremyan}, title = {{O}n the clique number of random {C}ayley graphs and related topics}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2412.21194}, note = {arXiv:2412.21194v1} }[Button: Copy Citation] ... As a further application of this result, we study a conjecture of Alon stating that every group of order $N$ has a Cayley graph whose clique number and independence number are both $O(\\log N)$, proving the conjecture for all abelian groups of order $N$ for almost all $N$.\n  * On the clique number of random Cayley graphs and related topics\n\nDavid Conlon, Jacob Fox, Huy Tuan Pham, Liana Yepremyan\n\nDec 31 2024 math.CO cs.DM math.NT math.PR arXiv:2412.21194v1\n\n0\n\n@misc{2412.21194, author = {David Conlon and Jacob Fox and Huy Tuan Pham and Liana Yepremyan}, title = {{O}n the clique number of random {C}ayley graphs and related topics}, year = {2024}, eprint = {2412.21194}, note = {arXiv:2412.21194v1} }[Button: Copy Citation]\n\nPDF\n\nWe prove that a random Cayley graph on a group of order $N$ has clique number $O(\\log N \\log \\log N)$ with high probability. This bound is best possible up to the constant factor for certain groups, including~$\\mathbb{F}_2^n$, and improves the longstanding upper bound of $O(\\log^2 N)$ due to Alon. Our proof does not make use of the underlying group structure and is purely combinatorial, with the key result being an essentially best possible upper bound for the number of subsets of given order that contain at most a given number of colors in a properly edge-colored complete graph. As a further application of this result, we study a conjecture of Alon stating that every group of order $N$ has a Cayley graph whose clique number and independence number are both $O(\\log N)$, proving the conjecture for all abelian groups of order $N$ for almost all $N$. For finite vector spaces of order $N$ with characteristic congruent to $1 \\pmod 4$, we prove the existence of a self-complementary Cayley graph on the vector space whose clique number and independence number are both at most $(2+o(1))\\log N$. This matches the lower bound for Ramsey numbers coming from random graphs and solves, in a strong form, a problem of Alon and Orlitsky motivated by information theory.\n\n  * Set-coloring Ramsey numbers and error-correcting codes near the zero-rate threshold\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHePhySTO – HEidelberg PHYsics Seminar Talks Organizer (https://www.physik.uni-heidelberg.de/hephysto/index/tools/createEventICS/index.php?id=12627&s=talk)\nciteturn9search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; [https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.21194](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.21194) David Conlon, Jacob Fox, Huy Tuan Pham, Liana Yepremyan ... 26 May 2026, 13:00 ... Disproof of the Odd Hadwiger Conjecture\n\nSTEPHEN PHILLIPS hostreviews.co.uk / UNSPLASH\n\n### On the clique number of random Cayley graphs and related topics\n\nJohn Larkin Image\n\n[https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.21194](https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.21194) David Conlon, Jacob Fox, Huy Tuan Pham, Liana Yepremyan\n\nDiscrete Mathematics Reading Group\n26 May 2026, 13:00\nLocation not specified.\nAdd to calendar\n\nToday:\n\nDisproof of the Odd Hadwiger Conjecture\nCynthia Xie\n13:00\nUncovering biophysical mechanisms in morphogenesis\nJulia Eckert\n16:00\nLandau-Zener Spektroskopie of atomic tunneling systems\nArpan Gauai\n16:00\nUsing proof assistants as more than checkers, and how to write papers about it\nYannick Forster\n16:15\nSolving Inverse Problems in Astronomy with Invertible Neural Networks\nVictor Ksoll\n16:30\nGamma-ray Astronomy and the Origins of Galactic Cosmic Rays\nDr. Alison Mitchell\n17:00\n\nUpcoming:\n\nExcited atoms&molecules in strong fields: Probing Electronic–Vibrational Coupling with Mid-IR – XUV Spectroscopy\nDr. Pankaj Seliya\nWed, 17 Jun 2026, 09:30\nMasures associated with split Kac-Moody groups over va